Logs the timestamps that decide whether a record reaches the particle filter. Not part of the build.

Apply from the repository root with:

    git apply local-loop/patches/gate-probe.patch

then run with -Doba.gateprobe=true and grep the engine log for "GATE".

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
Records are dropped between dispatch and inference by several checks, and none of them is counted. The
driver reports a dropped record as dispatched, so "read=270 dispatched=270" is consistent with any
number of records never reaching the filter. This makes the decision visible.

Each line carries: the device timestamp, the timestamp stamped from the replay clock, which of the two
getBestTimestamp chose, the filter's last-update time, and whether a previous observation exists.
GATE-ACTION lines mark the paths that consume a record and produce nothing.

WHAT IT FOUND
-------------
Comparing two four-thread runs of the same 270-record fixture: the clock stamp and the chosen timestamp
were byte-identical on every record, and record order within each bus was identical - which eliminated
the shared clock and record reordering as causes. No GATE-ACTION fired, so none of the instrumented drop
paths was responsible either.

What differed was the filter's own state: on 3 of 270 records the last-update time read as 0 when it
should have carried the previous record's timestamp. For three of five buses, particle creation
therefore ran on a different record in each run. Candidate counts at that record were identical
(40/40, 9/9, 51/51), so candidate generation was not the cause.

That is where the investigation stopped. The divergence is inside VehicleInferenceInstance's
initialisation path, and no reset or eviction call explains it - ParticleFilter.reset() was never
called, and all five callers of resetVehicleLocation were eliminated.

